 ## Overview
-This method reconstructs a triangle mesh from a Signed Distance Function (SDF) by evaluating the SDF on a regular grid of cubes, filtering out cubes that are too far from the surface (narrow-band filtering), and applying the standard Marching Cubes algorithm only to the intersected cubes [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard Marching Cubes by skipping the vast majority of the domain volume, significantly reducing computation time for sparse or bounded surfaces [S1, unofficial].
+This method reconstructs a triangle mesh from a Signed Distance Function (SDF) by evaluating the field on a grid of cubes, filtering out cubes that are too far from the surface (narrow-band filtering), and applying the standard Marching Cubes algorithm to the remaining cubes [S1, unofficial]. It differs from standard Marching Cubes by skipping the vast majority of voxels that do not intersect the isosurface, significantly reducing computational cost for sparse or bounded domains [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## How it works
 **Inputs:**
-- `domain`: A bounding box defined by `min` (Vec3) and `size` (Vec3).
-- `sdf`: A function `Fun3s` returning a scalar distance for any 3D position.
-- `cubeSize`: The dimensions of each grid cell (Vec3). If not provided, defaults to `domain.size / 50` [S1, unofficial].
-- `isoLevel`: The isovalue for surface extraction, defaulting to 0 [S1, unofficial].
-- `sdfGrad`: An optional gradient function for vertex normals. If `nullptr`, numerical approximation is used [G].
+*   `sdf`: A function $S: \mathbb{R}^3 \to \mathbb{R}$ returning the signed distance to the surface.
+*   `domain`: A bounding box defined by `min` and `size` vectors.
+*   `cubeSize`: The dimensions of the cubic grid cells.
+*   `isoLevel`: The scalar value at which the surface is extracted (typically 0 for SDFs) [S1, unofficial].
+*   `sdfGrad`: (Optional) A function returning the gradient $\nabla S$ at a point. If null, numerical approximation is used [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Outputs:**
-- `mesh`: A triangle mesh containing vertices and indices.
+*   A triangle mesh (vertices and indices) representing the isosurface [S1, unofficial].
 
 **Data Structures:**
